++  "body": "Thank you for you interest in strace. Note that discussions usually happen on the [mailing list](https://lists.strace.io/mailman/listinfo/strace-devel), so it probably makes sense to also write there, for the wider outreach.\r\n\r\nOverall, the idea has been being in the air for quite a long time, but there are several significant hurdles on the path to its realisation:\r\n * The need of API and the associated maintenance costs. Currently, various parts of strace that deemed to be API-worth are changed from time to time, and it's usually harmless process, but with a library it would be much more cumbersome process. Thinking the API through and implementing appropriate changes to make it at least somewhat future-proof is the main issue (but we can probably go with separating library part first and then stabilise API at some point bu declaring soname 1.0.0).\r\n * The format of the data. As of now, it's just strings. Of course, application that use strace want something more structured, and there were [some efforts](https://github.com/lineprinter/strace) to implement structured output in strace, but it's not here yet. And again, there's question of output format stabilisation, more so in the string form, as it's changed from time to time.\r\n * Global state. There are lots of global variables used in strace currently, and some functions lack any kind of handle (like struct tcb) to eliminate them. This is mostly technical issue, however.\r\n\r\nSo, without those bits implemented first I think it's unlikely that strace library is possible/useful, and so far there's no one in sight to implement those in a timely fashion, but otherwise strace project is slowly moving in that direction.",
