++  "updated_at": "2018-09-16T09:10:00Z",
++  "body": "Clang does in fact suppress warnings in headers included through `-isystem` paths, but does not suppress such warnings in macros expanded from system headers. Neither does GCC always do it, with exceptions added on case-by-case basis; excerpt from GCC docs:\r\n\r\n> Macros defined in a system header are immune to a few warnings wherever they are expanded. This immunity is granted on an ad-hoc basis, when we find that a warning generates lots of false positives because of code in macros defined in system headers.\r\n\r\nThe macro `NLMSG_LEN` is defined as follows:\r\n\r\n```\r\n#define NLMSG_OK(nlh,len) ((len) >= (int)sizeof(struct nlmsghdr) && \\\r\n                           (nlh)->nlmsg_len >= sizeof(struct nlmsghdr) && \\\r\n                           (nlh)->nlmsg_len <= (len))\r\n```\r\n\r\nSo, explicitly casting the second argument of `NLMSG_OK` to `__u32` would do the job.",
++  "author_association": "NONE",
