++  "body": "the only thing that comes to mind is to extend the `when`, but the problem is that it then requires escaping, as the equality is the minimum feature necessary and that's usually achieved by `==`\r\n\r\nso just brainstorming now:\r\n * `:when='arg0==1'` - applying the rule only when first argument is numerically equal to 1\r\n * `:when='arg0!=1'` - applying the rule only when first argument is numerically non-equal to 1\r\n * `:when='*arg0==\"/path/name\"'` - applying the rule when the first argument is a pointer to null terminated string equal to `/path/name`\r\n * `:when='*arg0=~\"name\"'` - applying the rule when the first argument matches the regexp `name`\r\n * `:when='arg1&O_APPEND'` - applying the rule when the second argument matches the `O_APPEND` flag (from `open(2)`)\r\n * `:when='!arg1&O_APPEND'` - applying the rule when the second argument does not have the flag matching the `O_APPEND` set\r\n * `:when='arg2>64'` - applying the rule when the third argument numerical value is bigger than 64\r\n * `:when='arg2>64 && arg2<=128'` applying the rule when third argument is bigger than 64 and less or equal to 128\r\n\r\n(not saying that all should be implemented, but having a plan means it's extensible in the future)",
++  "author_association": "NONE",
