++  "body": "While looking at one of these failed test, the syslog test.\r\n\r\n```\r\n+ read -r t prog_args\r\n+ '[' syslog = syslog ']'\r\n+ try_run_prog ../syslog\r\n+ local rc\r\n+ ../syslog\r\n+ run_strace -qq -esignal=none -e/clock ../syslog\r\n+ args='-qq -esignal=none -e/clock ../syslog'\r\n+ ../../src/strace -o log -qq -esignal=none -e/clock ../syslog\r\nCPU time limit exceeded\r\n++ dump_log_and_fail_with 'time limit (1500) exceeded'\r\n++ cat\r\n++ fail_ 'time limit (1500) exceeded'\r\n++ warn_ 'clock.gen.test: failed test: time limit (1500) exceeded'\r\n++ printf '%s\\n' 'clock.gen.test: failed test: time limit (1500) exceeded'\r\nclock.gen.test: failed test: time limit (1500) exceeded\r\n++ exit 1\r\nFAIL clock.gen.test (exit status: 124)\r\n```\r\n\r\nI tried to run `syslog` program manually, and attach the process with strace. I found out that it stuck at `syslog(SYSLOG_ACTION_READ,`.\r\n\r\n```\r\n09:38:20.987716 syslog(SYSLOG_ACTION_READ, \"\", 0) = 0 <0.000011>\r\n09:38:20.987764 write(1, \"syslog(2 /* SYSLOG_ACTION_READ *\"..., 46syslog(2 /* SYSLOG_ACTION_READ */, \"\", 0) = 0\r\n) = 46 <0.000015>\r\n09:38:20.987833 syslog(SYSLOG_ACTION_READ,\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf I write some data to kernel's printk buffer, `syslog` will continue running and exit normally. How can I make it pass the test without manually write data to kernel buffer?",
