++  "updated_at": "2021-09-04T09:21:44Z",
++  "body": "> If you're interested in immediate children only, then `strace -f -p \"$parent\" -p \"$(pgrep -P \"$parent\")\"` should suffice; otherwise, something like `strace -f -p \"$(l=\"$parent\"; while [ -n \"$l\" ]; do echo $l; n=\"\"; for i in $(echo \"$l\"); do; n=\"$n $(pgrep -P \"$i\")\"; done; l=\"$n\"; done)\"` is needed. I don't think the need to use a shell one-liner to build a list of processes of interest justifies tens and possibly hundreds of lines of code of finicky `procfs` manipulation required to implement such logic inside `strace`, especially consider all other possible PID selection needs covered by `pgrep` (same UID/GID/EUID/EGID/SUID/SGID/FSUID/FSGID/PGID/SID/PIDNS only? Direct children only? Siblings? Ancestors? How many levels?). Having an option to recursively select children in `pgrep` might be more straightforward though, considering the fact it already implements all the other possible process selection filters.\r\n\r\nin reality a single line is needed:\r\nthis one gets all children of a process..\r\n```\r\npstree -pn `pidof THEPROGRAM` |grep -o \"([[:digit:]]*)\" |grep -o \"[[:digit:]]*\"\r\n```\r\nso I think it's not a huge mess just to add that in the command line options.. \r\n",
