++  "body": "It's clear that the intent of this test code is to feed the kernel invalid input, presumably to ensure that strace will \r\nhandle both valid and invalid code, based on this commit:\r\n```\r\ncommit c965bec3a11671c51885996101e286bc27151b07\r\nAuthor: Eugene Syromyatnikov <evgsyr@gmail.com>\r\nDate:   Tue Aug 30 16:30:37 2016 +0300\r\n\r\n    tests: check decoding of futex syscall\r\n\r\n    * tests/futex.c: New file.\r\n    * tests/futex.test: New test.\r\n    * tests/.gitignore: Add futex.\r\n    * tests/Makefile.am (check_PROGRAMS): Likewise.\r\n    (DECODER_TESTS): Add futex.test.\r\n```\r\nIt seems like the kernel developers actually made a poor choice in accepting this bogus input, likely missing the fact that\r\na deliberate valid test checking for invalid data was being fed to the kernel. This is still the case in the latest kernel (commit a046f1a0d3e32)\r\n\r\n\r\nPerhaps the strace developers (original test written by @esyr) should discuss these warnings with the kernel developers to see if:\r\n- the messages can be removed \r\n- the message log can be updated to better show that it's only a warning\r\nFor example rather than:\r\n`futex_wake_op: futex tries to shift op by -849; fix this program`\r\nsomething along the lines of:\r\n`futex_wake_op: Warning: futex tries to shift op by -849`\r\n\r\nI am working with Randy MacLeod over the summer and we came across this bug when running the strace tests in the context of the Yocto project (https://bugzilla.yoctoproject.org/show_bug.cgi?id=13785)",
