++  "body": "> Thanks for this semi-magical tool!\r\n\r\nYou are welcome!\r\n\r\n> Strace doesn't support value `0x3001` as the subfunction to `arch_prctl` system call, and prints `\"ARCH_???\"` instead. It looks like it is a new glibc feature that utilizes shadow stack: https://codebrowser.dev/glibc/glibc/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/x86/include/asm/prctl.h.html\r\n\r\nNote that these ARCH_CET_* constants are defined in a glibc header file, not a kernel one, and the glibc header file starts with the  following comment:\r\n\r\n```\r\n/* FIXME: CET arch_prctl bits should come from the kernel header files.\r\n   This file should be removed if <asm/prctl.h> from the required kernel\r\n   header files contains CET arch_prctl bits.  */\r\n```\r\n\r\n> Any chance it is going to be added in the near future?\r\n\r\nApparently, this CET feature has never been merged into mainline linux kernel.\r\nThere were numerous attempts during the last years, the latest seems to be\r\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20230218211433.26859-1-rick.p.edgecombe@intel.com/T/#u\r\nwhere these constants have different names and numbers:\r\n\r\n```\r\n/* Don't use 0x3001-0x3004 because of old glibcs */\r\n\r\n#define ARCH_SHSTK_ENABLE\t\t0x5001\r\n#define ARCH_SHSTK_DISABLE\t\t0x5002\r\n#define ARCH_SHSTK_LOCK\t\t\t0x5003\r\n```\r\n\r\nGiven that 0x3001..0x3004 constants are rejected by the kernel people already, I think strace shouldn't support them either.",
