++  "updated_at": "2023-04-22T23:23:20Z",
++  "body": "I received helpful and encouraging remarks in response to my pull request\r\nfor adding a new feature.\r\nHowever, there were some bugs in the strace documentation, `make check`\r\nfailed, and I could not do a \"local run\" in my own environment of the\r\nworkflow for continuous integration.  I became discouraged.\r\n\r\nExecutive summary [TL;DR]:\r\nStrace project was not friendly to me as a new contributor because of \"silly\" errors.\r\n1. `make check` fails because `--enable-bundled=yes` is not configured.\r\n2. `--enable-code-coverage` is omitted, yet is required of an accepted patch.\r\n3. Parallel `make` is not mentioned, but saves HOURS of time for `make check`.\r\n4. No `clang-format` style file in source, despite a call to follow coding style.\r\n5. No mention of the need to use a Debian-derived system to run workflow.\r\n\r\nDetails:\r\nThe bugs start at the very beginning.  Both the `INSTALL` file and\r\nhttps://strace.io/wiki/NewContributorGuide say:\r\n```\r\n   ./bootstrap\r\n   ./configure\r\n   make\r\n   make check\r\n   make install\r\n```\r\nThat does not work as of commit 7a289e79bb5f14e91c874a3b5429e072e7b50bbd which was top-of-trunk on master branch on April 1. `make check` fails to compile all the tests:\r\n```\r\nIn file included from perf_event_open_unabbrev.c:2:\r\nperf_event_open.c: In function \u2018print_event_attr\u2019:\r\nperf_event_open.c:393:21: error: \u2018struct perf_event_attr\u2019 has no member named \u2018config3\u2019; did you mean \u2018config\u2019?\r\n  393 |         val = attr->config3;\r\n      |                     ^~~~~~~\r\n      |                     config\r\nmake[4]: *** [Makefile:16167: perf_event_open_unabbrev.o] Error 1\r\n```\r\nOn April 2, I ran on a pre-release beta of Fedora 38 running kernel 6.2.9\r\nand associated *.h files.  Later I updated to fedora 6.2.10-300.fc38.x86_64\r\nfrom April 6, and got the same error.  The strace source for\r\n`perf_event_open_unabbrev.c` assumed an even more-recent kernel version.\r\nI found `--enable-bundled=yes`.  That worked, and should have been mentioned\r\nas required for` ./configure`.  Also, `--enable-code-coverage` should be\r\nmentioned in order to enable guidance about required code coverage.\r\n\r\nThen I tried `make check`, and waited over two hours for it to finish.\r\nLater I added  `-j4`, which reduced the time to 46 minutes.\r\n(My machine has 4 CPU, 8 GB RAM, 2 MB L2 cache, SSD; /usr/bin/top showed\r\nno swap space used, maximum 1850 MB RAM used.)  Friendly directions should\r\nmention `-jN`, or explain why it should not be used.\r\n\r\nThe directions for submitting a patch call out coding style, but the\r\nsource contains no actual style file for clang-format.  \"[Linux] kernel\r\nstyle, but with some changes\" is not specific enough.\r\n\r\nThen I looked for the actual code coverage checks, and more generally\r\nfor the workflow for Continuous Integration.  A Debian-derived system\r\nis required (`apt-get`, etc.), but is not mentioned in documentation.\r\n",
