++  "body": "> In line with your changes in #263, we shouldn't be using `time_t`, neither in the decoder nor in the test of this syscall. As the kernel uses `__kernel_old_time_t` which is the same as `kernel_long_t`, we should rather use `kernel_long_t`.\r\n> \r\n> There might be a difficulty testing this on architectures where sizeof(long) < sizeof(kernel_long_t) such as x32 because `syscall` function may truncate the return value to fit `long`. In tests/times.c there is a workaround for a similar problem.\r\n\r\nShould that be a separate ticket and PR? This change is specifically about making the test match the code being tested. I have discovered the issue on a 32 bit system (with 64 bit time_t) after setting time to 2040, when the test starts expecting a negative value, but strace still prints a positive one.",
