++  "body": "> The errors reported from CI/CD checks are not related to this pull request.\r\n\r\nEven though the CI regression is completely unrelated to this PR, you might have better understanding what's the issue behind it and what could be a workaround.  These CI errors are new, they happen consistently on x32 runners, and the failing test is ioctl_kvm_run_auxstr_vcpu:\r\n```\r\n--- exp\t2023-11-20 06:19:59.114039139 +0000\r\n+++ out\t2023-11-20 06:19:59.114039139 +0000\r\n@@ -12,6 +12,6 @@\r\n ioctl(5<anon_inode:kvm-vcpu:0>, KVM_GET_SREGS, {cs={base=0xffff0000, limit=65535, selector=61440, type=10, present=1, dpl=0, db=0, s=1, l=0, g=0, avl=0}, ...}) = 0\r\n ioctl(5<anon_inode:kvm-vcpu:0>, KVM_SET_SREGS, {cs={base=0, limit=65535, selector=0, type=10, present=1, dpl=0, db=0, s=1, l=0, g=0, avl=0}, ...}) = 0\r\n ioctl(5<anon_inode:kvm-vcpu:0>, KVM_SET_REGS, {rax=0x2, ..., rsp=0, rbp=0, ..., rip=0x1000, rflags=0x2}) = 0\r\n-ioctl(5<anon_inode:kvm-vcpu:0>, KVM_RUN, 0) = 0 (KVM_EXIT_IO)\r\n-ioctl(5<anon_inode:kvm-vcpu:0>, KVM_RUN, 0) = 0 (KVM_EXIT_HLT)\r\n+ioctl(5<anon_inode:kvm-vcpu:0>, KVM_RUN, 0) = 0\r\n+ioctl(5<anon_inode:kvm-vcpu:0>, KVM_RUN, 0) = 0\r\n +++ exited with 0 +++\r\n```",
