++  "body": "> So a few tests failed but I cannot get a clear idea as to why given the nature of the changes.\r\n> \r\n> In `clang15-x86-kheaders`, `filter_seccomp-exitkill` failed. In `gcc13-x86_64-unwind` and `clang-x86_64-unwind`, `kill-on-exit.test`, `strace-k.test`, `strace-k-p.test`, `strace-k-demangle.test`.\r\n\r\nThis seems to be unrelated to the change.  For some unexplained reason, filter_seccomp-exitkill and kill-on-exit tests tend to hang up in github ci from time to time.  strace-k and strace-k-demangle tests didn't fail before, so there might be some subtle change in the environment provided by github ci runners.\r\n\r\n> > That's because include/uapi/linux/magic.h defines EXT2_SUPER_MAGIC, not EXT_SUPER_MAGIC.\r\n> \r\n> It defines `EXT3/4_SUPER_MAGIC` also: https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/de927f6c0b07d9e698416c5b287c521b07694cac/include/uapi/linux/magic.h#L24-L27. And in `strace` the values are only here for the user to have an idea of what is being passed, correct ? Would it not make sense to have it display a more general EXT value rather than a specific EXT2 value on all the ext derivatives ?\r\n\r\nstrace shows those symbolic names that are provided by the header files, so that user can look for them in the headers.\r\nIf there are several synonyms like EXT{2,3,4}_SUPER_MAGIC strace generally prefers to show the oldest of them.",
