++  "body": "@esyr Thanks for your feedback.  I'm happy to update this to suit how you all want this to work.  Some questions, though:\r\n\r\nAs currently implemented, `ioctl_decode_unknown_type()` would only show the hex contents of arg if all of the following are true:\r\n1. `_IOC_SIZE(code)` is non-zero\r\n2. The arg value is a valid pointer\r\n3. Reading `_IOC_SIZE(code)` bytes at address `arg` would not segfault\r\n\r\nOtherwise, the arg value itself would be printed.  That is to say that (1) the ioctl number needs to be encoded consistent with the intent that arg be a real buffer, and (2,3) the arg has to actually be a real buffer for strace to interpret / print it as such.  I think maybe this would weigh in favor of having the hex-dump behavior be enabled by default?  If you agree with that conclusion, what would you like the `-e` expression to be for force-printing the argument pointer instead?\r\n\r\nI could see a possibility where some users might want to know the pointer value instead of (or in addition to) the hex contents, if for example they're trying to understand why the wrong content is being passed to/from an ioctl (bad pointer math, etc.).  In an early draft of these changes, I considered trying to print both the pointer and the contents, but doing so didn't seem to follow any existing conventions that I could find in strace, so I set that idea aside.  Let me know if you think that approach warrants reconsideration, though.\r\n\r\nOf course, please let me know if I'm misunderstanding your position or leaving out something important that you'd like to see changed.",
