++  "body": "Suppose that you want to trace a process and that process launches a child process that uses a setuid binary.  Running with `strace` works because it doesn't trace the child processes.  Running with `strace -f` (perhaps to trace threads in the main process) will not work properly.  It will trace the child process and then the kernel will decide to not make the process setuid, which is likely to break the child process.\n\n`strace -f -b execve` seems like it is designed for just this purpose, but it also fails.  The `execve` happens first, the kernel decides not to make the process setuid, and then strace detaches.  Here's a concrete example.  You'll need to provide some username in place of `$SOME_OTHER_USER`.  You can use `root` if you want to, but using a dummy user is probably more advisable.\n\n```Shell\n$ mkdir strace_setuid\n$ cd strace_setuid\n$ cat > mysetuid.c <<EOF\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    const int uid = getuid();\n    const int euid = geteuid();\n    if (euid == uid) {\n        puts(\"setuid ignored\");\n        return 1;\n    }\n    puts(\"setuid working\");\n    return 0;\n}\nEOF\n\n$ chmod go+w .\n$ sudo -u $SOME_OTHER_USER sh -c 'gcc -Wall mysetuid.c -o mysetuid && chmod +s mysetuid'\n\n# Verify that the program runs setuid when run directly\n$ ./mysetuid\nsetuid working\n\n# We can successfully trace a process that launches mysetuid as a subprocess\n$ strace -o /dev/null sh -c './mysetuid; exit'\nsetuid working\n\n# Using `-f` will fail, as expected\n$ strace -o /dev/null -f sh -c './mysetuid; exit'\nsetuid ignored\n\n# Using `-f -b execve` doesn't fix it\n$ strace -o /dev/null -f -b execve sh -c './mysetuid; exit'\nsetuid ignored\n```\n\nI can hack around this with a patch like this:\n\n```Patch\ndiff --git a/src/strace.c b/src/strace.c\nindex ee80de0..71571db 100644\n--- a/src/strace.c\n+++ b/src/strace.c\n@@ -43,6 +43,7 @@\n #include \"delay.h\"\n #include \"wait.h\"\n #include \"secontext.h\"\n+#include \"sen.h\"\n \n /* In some libc, these aren't declared. Do it ourself: */\n extern char **environ;\n@@ -4022,6 +4023,15 @@ dispatch_event(const struct tcb_wait_data *wd)\n \t\t\t */\n \t\t\treturn true;\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif (detach_on_execve &&\n+\t\t\texiting(current_tcp) &&  /* actually entering */\n+\t\t\t(tcp_sysent(current_tcp)->sen == SEN_execve ||\n+\t\t\t tcp_sysent(current_tcp)->sen == SEN_execveat)) {\n+\t\t\tif (!(current_tcp->flags & TCB_SKIP_DETACH_ON_FIRST_EXEC)) {\n+\t\t\t\tdetach(current_tcp); /* do \"-b execve\" thingy */\n+\t\t\t\treturn true;\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n \t\tif (has_seccomp_filter(current_tcp)) {\n \t\t\t/*\n \t\t\t * Syscall and seccomp stops can happen in different\n```\n\nI assume that there must be a reason that things aren't already being done this way.  Maybe because this will detach even if the `execve` call fails?  The manual page doesn't really specify that strace only detaches on a successful `execve` and I guess from reading the manual I would have assumed that it detaches on any `execve`, not just successful ones.  That's not the current behavior, AFAICT.\n\n\nAnother alternative may be to add an option similar to valgrind's `--trace-children-skip=` (https://valgrind.org/docs/manual/manual-core.html#opt.trace-children-skip).  This would allow the explicit listing of any setuid processes that the user did not want to trace into.  It would be a bit inconvenient as it would be left to the user to discover all needed path(s) to specify.  But it would allow tracing of all other child processes, which could be useful.\n\n\nI was going to suggest maybe looking at the binary to be executed and seeing if it was setuid and automatically detaching if it was.  Since filesystem capabilities are becoming more popular, just looking at the setuid bit probably isn't enough anymore.  Figuring out all the different things that might need to be checked could be complicated and maybe that's too much to expect strace to do.\n\n\n",
