++  "body": "## Setup\n\nThis bug can be reproduced on x86_64 Linux with various strace versions >= 6.6\n\n## Description\n\nWhen configuring `--with-libunwind` (versions >= 1.8.0), and running with `-k`, the execution stack trace will not include function names for which the symbols have size larger than 40 characters, which is a [hardcoded limit when walking the stack](https://github.com/strace/strace/blob/ba20408d99c391c937453807285394117f8f3da7/src/unwind-libunwind.c#L119).\n\nIn my understanding, the expectation seems to be: in case this size isn't enough to fill a buffer with the found name, that `unw_get_proc_name()` would return `-UNW_ENOMEM`, and the [buffer would be adjusted](https://github.com/strace/strace/blob/ba20408d99c391c937453807285394117f8f3da7/src/unwind-libunwind.c#L61). However, due to what seems like a [libunwind bug](https://github.com/libunwind/libunwind/issues/922), this error code is no longer returned, and instead, we always get a single error `-UNW_ENOINFO`.\n\nI'm also reporting this issue here for awareness, and in case it makes sense to implement some best effort workaround for these cases:\n\n* Check if the buffer was filled after the call to `unw_get_proc_name()`, even if `-UNW_ENOINFO` is returned, and either print the (truncated) buffer contents, or fall-through to `xgrowarray()`;\n* Allow `symbol_name_size` to be configurable (similar to what was done for `--stack-trace-frame-limit`), as any size that is large enough to cover the largest symbol name present will avoid this issue altogether, since `unw_get_proc_name()` will always successfully fill the provided buffer.\n\n## Example (x86_64)\n\na.c:\n```c\nvoid a123456789_123456789_123456789_123456789() {\n    asm(\"movl $0x3c,%eax\\nsyscall\\n\");\n}\nvoid _start() {\n    a123456789_123456789_123456789_123456789();\n}\n```\n\nObserved output (affected frame has no symbol name, and offset is `+0x0`):\n```\ngcc -O0 -nostdlib -static a.c && strace -k ./a.out\n\nexecve(\"./a.out\", [\"./a.out\"], 0x7ffc6a012908 /* 145 vars */) = 0\n > /tmp/a.out(_start+0x0) [0x100e]\nexit(0)                                 = ?\n+++ exited with 0 +++\n > /tmp/a.out(+0x0) [0x100b]\n > /tmp/a.out(_start+0xe) [0x101c]\n > unexpected_backtracing_error [0x1]\n```\n\nExpected output (frame with large symbol name and correct offset):\n```\n > /tmp/a.out(a123456789_123456789_123456789_123456789+0xb) [0x100b]\n```",
