++  "body": "Hi! I'm interested in adding support for decoding the ioctls used against `/dev/nvidia*`, and I figured I'd open an issue here before I write too much code and see if anyone else is interested in this.\n\nOn the userspace side, the ioctls are called by non-free libraries like CUDA (`libcuda.so`) and NVML (`libnvidia-ml.so`, the NVIDIA Management Library), and NVIDIA's GLX library. In turn, they are used by higher-level GPU users like Torch, `nvidia-smi`, and Xorg.\n\nOn the kernel side, the ioctls are implemented by both the more common non-free NVIDIA drivers and by the official open-source drivers at https://github.com/NVIDIA/open-gpu-kernel-modules. This repository has a handful of header files defining the interface as well as actual implementation for reference. My understanding is that the code is derived from a common core also used for the non-free drivers and other NVIDIA products.\n\nGoogle's \"gvisor\" hypervisor also implements these ioctls for the purpose of securely virtualizing access to the GPU, and in the process they track differences in the ABI across driver versions. They currently handle ABIs from driver versions 535 to 575, and if you look in history they previously handled driver version 525. (I have not looked closely to see what the ABI differences are. Also, this implementation seems to be targeted at the open source driver, although my vague sense is that compatibility is based on the driver version number reported by `__NV_IOWR(NV_ESC_CHECK_VERSION_STR)`, which I think is the same string.) https://github.com/google/gvisor/tree/master/pkg/sentry/devices/nvproxy\n\nNote that these are not the same as the `/dev/nvhost*` interface for which a patch was previously submitted at https://lists.strace.io/pipermail/strace-devel/2020-February/009344.html, which use ioctl type codes `'A`, `G`, `H` (overlaps with hiddev), `N`, and `T` (overlaps with term and soundcard). That seems to be the interface for the Tegra embedded/SOC GPUs. At the moment I'm most interested in (and have access to) the server-class GPUs (formerly \"Tesla\"), using ioctl type code `F` (no overlap).\n\nI started hacking on this over at geofft/strace branch nvidia, which gets me at least ioctl names:\n```\n$ strace -P /dev/nvidiactl nvidia-smi 2>&1 >/dev/null | head\nstat(\"/dev/nvidiactl\", {st_mode=S_IFCHR|0666, st_rdev=makedev(0xc3, 0xff), ...}) = 0\nstat(\"/dev/nvidiactl\", {st_mode=S_IFCHR|0666, st_rdev=makedev(0xc3, 0xff), ...}) = 0\nopenat(AT_FDCWD, \"/dev/nvidiactl\", O_RDWR) = 9\nfcntl(9, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC)           = 0\nioctl(9, _IOC(_IOC_READ|_IOC_WRITE, 0x46, 0xd2, 0x48), 0x7fffd85b31a0) = 0\nioctl(9, _IOC(_IOC_READ|_IOC_WRITE, 0x46, 0xd6, 0x8), 0x7fffd85b32b0) = 0\nioctl(9, _IOC(_IOC_READ|_IOC_WRITE, 0x46, 0xc8, 0x900), 0x79e3046cc0a0) = 0\nioctl(9, _IOC(_IOC_READ|_IOC_WRITE, 0x46, 0x2b, 0x20), 0x7fffd85b3490) = 0\nioctl(9, _IOC(_IOC_READ|_IOC_WRITE, 0x46, 0x2a, 0x20), 0x7fffd85b29f0) = 0\nioctl(9, _IOC(_IOC_READ|_IOC_WRITE, 0x46, 0x2a, 0x20), 0x7fffd85b29f0) = 0\n$ src/strace -P /dev/nvidiactl nvidia-smi 2>&1 >/dev/null | head\nstat(\"/dev/nvidiactl\", {st_mode=S_IFCHR|0666, st_rdev=makedev(0xc3, 0xff), ...}) = 0\nstat(\"/dev/nvidiactl\", {st_mode=S_IFCHR|0666, st_rdev=makedev(0xc3, 0xff), ...}) = 0\nopenat(AT_FDCWD, \"/dev/nvidiactl\", O_RDWR) = 9\nfcntl(9, F_SETFD, FD_CLOEXEC)           = 0\nioctl(9, __NV_IOWR(NV_ESC_CHECK_VERSION_STR), 0x7ffe3a970150) = 0\nioctl(9, __NV_IOWR(NV_ESC_SYS_PARAMS), 0x7ffe3a970260) = 0\nioctl(9, __NV_IOWR(NV_ESC_CARD_INFO), 0x7b7166acc0a0) = 0\nioctl(9, __NV_IOWR(NV_ESC_RM_ALLOC), 0x7ffe3a970440) = 0\nioctl(9, __NV_IOWR(NV_ESC_RM_CONTROL), 0x7ffe3a96f9a0) = 0\nioctl(9, __NV_IOWR(NV_ESC_RM_CONTROL), 0x7ffe3a96f9a0) = 0\n```\n\nI think my first question is how to handle the header files. They are MIT-licensed, so they _can_ be vendored into the repo if we want. I think I only see one example of consuming headers from outside the mainline kernel, for ZFS, is that correct? That one seems to look at the headers and generate `src/ioctls_zfs.h` which is checked in, but also that only handles ioctl naming (as above) and not the actual parameters. I'm likely to want the header files to decode the parameters into structs too. Is it preferable to vendor them and treat them more like mainline uapi is treated?",
