++  "body": "Currently output for \"call count report\" include: %time, seconds, usec/call, calls, error per syscall. Suggestion adding additional aggregate - \"**Time Spent in each call (seconds) **\". Either as extra column in the current `-c` report, or as option to extend the current report.\r\n\r\nThe \"Time Spent\" in each call is already available as an `-T` option to the 'detailed' report. it provides important value - it shows the cost of the system calls in terms of \"Wall time\", and not just in terms of kernel CPU time. The most obvious example will be IO calls (read, write, select, open, ...). When analyzing performance of programs - knowing the actual time to complete IO is extremely useful information.\r\n\r\nFrom my experience - only the cumulative value (in seconds) is relevant). No need to average, or % of total.\r\n\r\nMy current workaround is to running strace in the 'detailed' mode with `-T` - collecting the time from the <NNN> tokens at the end of each line (from the -T), and aggregating it by system call. See example blow: e.g. <0.000068> in the `read` system call. However this approach is inefficient - standard error of strace need to captured into a (large) file, and post-processed. It add lot of overhead to the measuring (compared with the light cost of the -c report).\r\n\r\n```\r\nread(0, \"4\\t./.cache\\n24\\t./proj/x\\n28\\t./proj\"..., 16384) = 4124 <0.000068>\r\nread(0, \"40260\\t./proj/ln-pairs/y\\n80520\\t./\"..., 16384) = 46 <0.002553>\r\nread(0, \"130896\\t./proj\\n2704\\t./bin\\n133708\\t\"..., 16384) = 34 <0.000069>\r\nread(0, \"\", 16384)                      = 0 <0.000061>\r\nnewfstatat(1, \"\", {st_mode=S_IFCHR|0620, st_rdev=makedev(0x88, 0), ...}, AT_EMPTY_PATH) = 0 <0.000059>\r\nwrite(1, \"    120     240    4204\\n\", 24    120     240    4204\r\n) = 24 <0.000085>\r\nclose(0)                                = 0 <0.000086>\r\nclose(1)                                = 0 <0.000073>\r\nclose(2)                                = 0 <0.000071>\r\nexit_group(0)                           = ?\r\n```\r\n",
