++  "body": "Prepare two termianls A and B on x86_64\r\n\r\nA:\r\n```\r\nA$ cat poll.c \r\n#include <poll.h>\r\n#include <unistd.h>\r\n#include <stdio.h>\r\n\r\nint\r\nmain(void)\r\n{\r\n  struct pollfd fds[1] = {{.fd = 0, .events = POLLIN,},};\r\n  fprintf(stderr, \"attach me: %d\\n\", getpid());\r\n  return poll(fds, 1, -1);\r\n}\r\nA$ gcc poll.c\r\nA$ ./a.out \r\nattach me: 2073429\r\n```\r\n\r\nB:\r\n```\r\nB$ ./src/strace -p 2073429      \r\n./src/strace: Process 2073429 attached\r\nrestart_syscall(<... resuming interrupted read ...>\r\n```\r\n\r\nstrace reported \"read\" instead of \"poll\".\r\n\r\nIf you run the a.out under strace, strace reports the calling poll correctly:\r\n```\r\nC$ ./src/strace /tmp/a.out\r\n...\r\ngetpid()                                = 2073828\r\nwrite(2, \"attach me: 2073828\\n\", 19attach me: 2073828\r\n)    = 19\r\npoll([{fd=0, events=POLLIN}], 1, -1\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nI tried tihs on\r\n```\r\n[yamato@dev64]~/var/strace% uname -r\r\n6.10.12-200.fc40.x86_64\r\n```\r\n",
