++  "body": "Currently when you use options `-y` to annotate file names and socckets in the syscalls arguments it works good for the regular files (and makes life much easier! :+1:) but it could be improved for UNIX and network sockets. Currently you can see: something like:\r\n```\r\naccept4(3<socket:[15157]>, {sa_family=AF_INET, sin_port=htons(62958), sin_addr=inet_addr(\"1.2.3.4\")}, [16], SOCK_CLOEXEC) = 29<socket:[91704707]>\r\nopen(\"/srv/www/i/moon.jpg\", O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC) = 30</srv/www/test.jpg>\r\n...\r\nsendfile(29<socket:[91704707]>, 30</srv/www/test.jpg>, [0], 21952) = 21952\r\n```\r\nSo it's not clear from the sendfile line, what is the 29-th file description (or socket's 91704707) destination is? It could be shown like this for better understanding:\r\n`sendfile(29<socket:[91704707,AF_INET,1.2.3.4:62958]>, 30</srv/www/test.jpg>, [0], 21952) = 21952`",
