++  "body": "On x86_64, strace is using linux' 64 bit syscall numbers to look up 32 bit syscalls made in a 64 bit binary. I'm using strace 5.1.\r\n\r\nCode to reproduce:\r\n```asm\r\n.section .text\r\n.global _start\r\n_start:\r\n\r\nmov $4, %eax\r\nmov $1, %ebx\r\nmov $hello, %ecx\r\nmov $14, %edx\r\nint $0x80\r\n\r\nmov $1, %eax\r\nmov $0, %ebx\r\nint $0x80\r\n\r\nhello: .ascii \"hello, world!\\n\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nCompile and run with\r\n```\r\nas --64 -o hello.o hello.S && ld -melf_x86_64 -o hello hello.o && strace ./hello\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n```\r\nexecve(\"./hello\", [\"./hello\"], 0x7ffc3412a900 /* 46 vars */) = 0\r\nstat(NULL, hello, world!\r\nNULL)                        = 14\r\nwrite(0, NULL, 14)                      = ?\r\n+++ exited with 0 +++\r\n```\r\nInstead of `write` and `exit`, strace detects `stat` and `write`.",
